4385 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "275009c1-6d24-5677-85a3-ec734133ac52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064386 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064386 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064386 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186523931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064386 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186523958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064386 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186523976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064386 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186523980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064386 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064386 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg as an Angle Varies" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064386 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186064030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064386 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bfd57a01-349d-5632-96a3-7f17a8e484d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064455 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064455 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064455 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064455 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064455 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064455 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064455 #edb/ref 1045 "Calculating Related Rates Using Trigonometry" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064455 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186065326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064455 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064455 #edb/ref 1050 1440.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064455 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "35485642-6f34-523f-ba9d-b8883802af55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065177 #edb/ref 1061 27273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065177 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9ce63c6b-93ff-55b3-823f-2b9e85238652" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065177 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065326 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065326 #edb/ref 1061 934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065326 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d3981e94-4f89-5b37-84f2-1d3e0cbb41c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065326 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065327 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065327 #edb/ref 1061 935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065327 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "72b9ba59-522f-50d2-bed7-143439adb60f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065327 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065328 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065328 #edb/ref 1061 939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065328 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "70026486-4a5b-5b48-bfe7-c78c5c312ef0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065328 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065329 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065329 #edb/ref 1061 940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065329 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "804402e9-5068-546f-a006-16edfa5e5a69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065329 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065387 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that a jet takes off and climbs with an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians to the horizontal. The speed of the jet is $462\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}$ and the angle of elevation does not change. How fast is the altitude of the jet increasing?\n\nLet's begin by drawing a diagram depicting the given information.\n\n![](images/70/708ba0a80dd7c8e3e58e1456ad643338a8e630ec45aedb8955b042ddac92c329.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the jet's height from the ground (i.e., its altitude), $z$ is the jet's distance from its take-off point. The quantity that we're required to calculate is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d z}{\\textrm d t} = 462\\,\\textrm{km/h},$ so plugging this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 462 = 231.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the altitude is increasing at a rate of $231\\,\\textrm{km/h}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065387 #edb/ref 1065 501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065387 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "da3b2021-8bcc-506c-8874-9ce60aca7594" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065387 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065388 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider a $2\\,\\text{m}$ ladder that leans against a wall, forming an angle $\\theta$ with the ground. The bottom of the ladder is pushed toward the wall at a rate of $\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},$ as shown below. How fast is the angle $\\theta$ increasing?\n\nLet's begin by drawing a figure according to the given information.\n\n![](images/16/16996176a8557dc71fe3f3a3e0d2a0c91e5b164b9eeeeeb6cf76b44952642a47.png)\n\nIn the figure,  $z$ is the length of the ladder and $x$ the distance of the bottom of the ladder from the wall. The quantity $z=2$ does not change with time because the length of the ladder is fixed.\n\nOur job is to calculate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\theta}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta,$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the bottom of the ladder is pushed against the wall at a rate of $\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},$ we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{1}{2},$ and therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065388 #edb/ref 1065 503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065388 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9abef4d4-d050-57eb-8e0a-61634d35af20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065388 #edb/ref 1067 "Calculating Rates of Varying Angles Using Trigonometry" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
